\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces  Reconstructed observed (left panel) and astrophysical (right panel) binary black hole population in log10 of component masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ using the hierarchical DPGMM implementation in FIGARO inferred from simulated population of gravitational-wave signals mimicking observing run O4. The diagonal panels show the one-dimensional marginal distributions, while the off-diagonal panels show the corresponding two-dimensional joint distributions. The observed population is biased towards higher masses due to selection effects, while the astrophysical population is corrected for these effects and represents the true underlying distribution of binary black hole mergers, extending to higher redshifts and lower masses that are relevant to strong gravitational-wave lensing, which occurs for binaries at high redshift. }}{16}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:reconstructed_population_log}{{5}{16}{Reconstructed observed (left panel) and astrophysical (right panel) binary black hole population in log10 of component masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ using the hierarchical DPGMM implementation in FIGARO inferred from simulated population of gravitational-wave signals mimicking observing run O4. The diagonal panels show the one-dimensional marginal distributions, while the off-diagonal panels show the corresponding two-dimensional joint distributions. The observed population is biased towards higher masses due to selection effects, while the astrophysical population is corrected for these effects and represents the true underlying distribution of binary black hole mergers, extending to higher redshifts and lower masses that are relevant to strong gravitational-wave lensing, which occurs for binaries at high redshift}{figure.5}{}}
